<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stefan Dečanski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1285–<a href="/years/1331">1331</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Stefan Uroš III Dečanski · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stefan Uroš III Dečanski was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1321 to 1331, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a>. He won a decisive victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-velbazhd">Battle of Velbazhd</a> in 1330, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-shishman">Michael Shishman</a> was killed. He founded the monastery of Visoki Dečani, from which his name comes, and was overthrown in 1331 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> and died soon afterwards in prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stefan-decanski.jpg" alt="Stefan Dečanski" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BLAGO Fund, Inc. · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stefan_Uro%C5%A1_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stefan Uroš III was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Uroš II Milutin</a> and his first wife Jelena, a Serbian noblewoman. While still a youth he was sent as a hostage to the court of Nogai Khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, to keep the peace between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, and stayed there until about 1297. By 1309 Milutin had made him governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>. In 1314 he quarrelled with his father, who sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to be blinded; he was never totally blinded and was probably not blinded at all. At the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronikos II Palaiologos</a> he wrote to Danilo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Hum, asking him to intercede, and in 1320 he was allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of Budimlje.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Milutin died on 29 October 1321 without formal instructions about the succession. His son Stefan Konstantin was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeta-region">Zeta</a>, while Dečanski and his cousin Stefan Vladislav II also claimed the throne. Dečanski revealed that his eyesight was intact and claimed a miracle, and the people rallied to him. On 6 January 1322 Archbishop Nicodemus crowned him king and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> young king. Konstantin was defeated and killed in Zeta. Vladislav, supported by Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and Bosnians, was defeated in battle in late 1324 and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. A dispute over Ragusan merchants who had backed Vladislav led Dečanski to seize their property in 1324, and he returned it by the end of that year.</p><p>In 1326 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> lost Hum to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. Dečanski generally kept an alliance with Andronikos II and stayed out of the Byzantine civil war. Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-shishman">Michael Asen III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, whose divorce from Dečanski's sister Anna was followed by his marriage to a Byzantine princess, planned a joint invasion of Serbia in 1330 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos">Andronikos III Palaiologos</a>. At the Battle of Velbazhd Dečanski defeated and killed Michael, with help from Dušan, and Andronikos withdrew. Serbia's border moved south into Byzantine Macedonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Some courtiers, discontented with his policies, conspired to put Dušan in his place. In 1331 Dušan came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Skadar</a> to Nerodimlje and Dečanski fled to Petrič, which Dušan took on 21 August 1331 after a siege. Dečanski was imprisoned in Zvečan Fortress and died there on 11 November 1331. Epic poetry shows him as a noble character, and the Serbian Orthodox Church canonised him. His remains are venerated at Visoki Dečani, where a supplicatory canon is chanted every Thursday and an Akathist daily.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stefan Dečanski” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stefan Dušan, son of King <b>Stefan Dečanski</b> (r. 1322–1331), held the title of young king and ruled the province of Zeta, as Serbian heirs traditionally did.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Serbian civil war, Stefan Dušan took Petrič after a siege and imprisoned his father, King <b>Stefan Uroš III Dečanski</b>, in Zvečan Fortress, where he died in November.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Milutin (1282–1321) took northern Macedonia from Byzantium, and <b>Stefan Dečanski</b> crushed Bulgaria at Velbazhd in 1330.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia-medieval">Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Zvečan Fortress, in what is now <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.9,20.84&amp;year=1314" aria-label="Zvečan Fortress on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5053 640 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Stefan Dečanski"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5503 910h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5503 910h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.9,20.84&amp;year=1314">What was here in 1314? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
